WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.334 --> 00:00:02.133
-Our first guest is
one of the co-hosts

00:00:02.133 --> 00:00:03.634
of "Good Morning America".

00:00:03.634 --> 00:00:07.133
He also has a new clothing line
called MSX by Michael Strahan.

00:00:07.133 --> 00:00:11.834
Here he is, the one
and only Michael Strahan.

00:00:11.834 --> 00:00:13.467
♪ Michael Strahan ♪

00:00:13.467 --> 00:00:15.801
♪ He's one of a kind ♪

00:00:15.801 --> 00:00:20.434
♪ Sharp as a razor,
he's got his own clothing line ♪

00:00:20.434 --> 00:00:24.601
♪ Michael Strahan
gonna make you laugh ♪

00:00:24.601 --> 00:00:29.701
♪ When we talk to him
about the NFL draft ♪

00:00:29.701 --> 00:00:30.968
-Unbelievable.

00:00:30.968 --> 00:00:32.701
-It is great. It is great
to see you.

00:00:32.701 --> 00:00:35.033
-Always, always,
you are the best.

00:00:35.033 --> 00:00:39.067
Thank you. You just crushed me
yet again at golf.

00:00:39.067 --> 00:00:40.567
-We had fun.
-[ Laughs ]

00:00:40.567 --> 00:00:41.601
-We had a lot of fun.

00:00:41.601 --> 00:00:43.667
I really thought
I was going to lose.

00:00:43.667 --> 00:00:45.801
-I was right.
-Because you're good.

00:00:45.801 --> 00:00:47.234
-No, I was not good at all.

00:00:47.234 --> 00:00:48.501
That was terrible.
That was awful.

00:00:48.501 --> 00:00:51.133
I didn't get one in at all.
It was terrible.

00:00:51.133 --> 00:00:52.367
-We could we could edit it.

00:00:52.367 --> 00:00:54.734
[ Laughter ]

00:00:54.734 --> 00:00:57.434
-Buddy, I want to talk to you
about a bunch of things,

00:00:57.434 --> 00:01:02.200
but first, the NFL draft
is coming up April 29th,

00:01:02.200 --> 00:01:05.934
and what what goes through --
since you've done this --

00:01:05.934 --> 00:01:08.467
what goes through your head
when you're knowing

00:01:08.467 --> 00:01:09.934
that you're going to get
drafted to a team.

00:01:09.934 --> 00:01:11.067
-I hope.

00:01:11.067 --> 00:01:12.767
Like, you hope
you get drafted.

00:01:12.767 --> 00:01:14.100
-Like, unless you're
Trevor Lawrence,

00:01:14.100 --> 00:01:15.234
you don't really know
where you're going to go.

00:01:15.234 --> 00:01:16.434
You know you're going to go
to Jacksonville,

00:01:16.434 --> 00:01:17.667
but other than that,

00:01:17.667 --> 00:01:19.701
you just hope you're going
to get drafted.

00:01:19.701 --> 00:01:21.534
You're praying you
can, like, make enough money

00:01:21.534 --> 00:01:23.767
so you don't have to live
with your parents anymore.

00:01:23.767 --> 00:01:25.968
And -- but then,
when you get drafted,

00:01:25.968 --> 00:01:27.434
it doesn't mean
that you've made it,

00:01:27.434 --> 00:01:29.634
and I think that's what a lot
of young players get mixed up.

00:01:29.634 --> 00:01:31.434
They think, "I got drafted,
I made it"

00:01:31.434 --> 00:01:32.801
-Right.
-They go get the car,

00:01:32.801 --> 00:01:34.934
the jewelry and everything else
that comes with it.

00:01:34.934 --> 00:01:37.501
No, that's just your opportunity
to be in the NFL.

00:01:37.501 --> 00:01:38.968
You know, you haven't made it.

00:01:38.968 --> 00:01:40.834
That's when your work
really starts,

00:01:40.834 --> 00:01:42.567
because if you don't
put it in the work,

00:01:42.567 --> 00:01:43.834
you won't be there long.

00:01:43.834 --> 00:01:45.901
And, you know,
with football careers,

00:01:45.901 --> 00:01:50.133
even when they are long like
mine -- I retired at 36.

00:01:50.133 --> 00:01:52.000
Yeah. You got a long life
to live afterwards, hopefully,

00:01:52.000 --> 00:01:53.667
so got to take care
of your money.

00:01:53.667 --> 00:01:55.534
Save your money, kids.
Save your money.

00:01:55.534 --> 00:01:56.734
-[ Laughs ] Exactly, yeah.

00:01:56.734 --> 00:01:58.734
-And put in all the work,
and you get out of it

00:01:58.734 --> 00:01:59.767
what you put into it.

00:01:59.767 --> 00:02:01.434
-Yeah.
-Plain and simple.

00:02:01.434 --> 00:02:03.167
-What do you remember
about the first day

00:02:03.167 --> 00:02:05.400
going into the locker room
for the Giants,

00:02:05.400 --> 00:02:07.133
or coming to New York --
what do you remember?

00:02:07.133 --> 00:02:08.701
-I was scared as hell.

00:02:08.701 --> 00:02:10.868
I was scared.
-Really?

00:02:10.868 --> 00:02:14.734
-I'm in this locker room.
I'm not, like, a big guy,

00:02:14.734 --> 00:02:16.734
but I'm watching
these grown men.

00:02:16.734 --> 00:02:19.167
-Yes, you are.
-I'm not a big guy.

00:02:19.167 --> 00:02:20.400
Have you seen
some of these guys?

00:02:20.400 --> 00:02:22.734
6'7", 6'8", Solid muscle.

00:02:22.734 --> 00:02:25.400
330, 40, 50 pounds.

00:02:25.400 --> 00:02:28.234
And then the guys who played
my position, one of these guys,

00:02:28.234 --> 00:02:30.901
he was so -- he had muscles
coming out of everywhere, neck,

00:02:30.901 --> 00:02:32.467
ears, everything.

00:02:32.467 --> 00:02:34.000
-Ear muscles?
-Yeah, ear muscles.

00:02:34.000 --> 00:02:36.634
And he walks in the --
he walks in the locker room.

00:02:36.634 --> 00:02:38.934
It's the first time I'd ever
been there with the vets,

00:02:38.934 --> 00:02:40.968
and he was a vet. He walks in
and I just remember

00:02:40.968 --> 00:02:44.501
looking at him thinking,
"He plays my position.

00:02:44.501 --> 00:02:47.601
If I have to look
like him to play,

00:02:47.601 --> 00:02:49.567
I might as well go home. I'm
never going to look like that.

00:02:49.567 --> 00:02:51.667
-Really?
-Ah, it was intimidating.

00:02:51.667 --> 00:02:53.501
So I remember being scared.

00:02:53.501 --> 00:02:55.667
I remember Lawrence Taylor
walking into the locker room...

00:02:55.667 --> 00:02:57.701
-Yeah! LT, man.

00:02:57.701 --> 00:03:00.133
-...And it was like he didn't
even walk, he glided in

00:03:00.133 --> 00:03:01.234
like he was
on a conveyor belt.

00:03:01.234 --> 00:03:02.367
-[ Laughs ]

00:03:02.367 --> 00:03:04.400
-Yeah, he floated in.
-He was so like...

00:03:04.400 --> 00:03:06.567
He was such a god to me
in football.

00:03:06.567 --> 00:03:09.467
So, yeah, it was --
it was intimidating,

00:03:09.467 --> 00:03:11.467
but the sooner you can get over
and realize you're there

00:03:11.467 --> 00:03:14.601
because you deserve to be there
and that you belong,

00:03:14.601 --> 00:03:15.601
then the better
it'll be for you.

00:03:15.601 --> 00:03:16.801
But it's intimidating at first.

00:03:16.801 --> 00:03:19.267
-And did you feel comfortable
in New York City?

00:03:19.267 --> 00:03:20.334
-No.

00:03:20.334 --> 00:03:22.067
No. The first time I came here,

00:03:22.067 --> 00:03:23.501
I stayed in
the Marriott Marquis,

00:03:23.501 --> 00:03:24.934
right in Times Square.
-Yeah.

00:03:24.934 --> 00:03:28.067
-I didn't leave my room
until somebody came and got me.

00:03:28.067 --> 00:03:30.467
Because I'm not that big, Jimmy.

00:03:30.467 --> 00:03:32.634
-Yes, you are!
You are that big!

00:03:32.634 --> 00:03:35.067
-I'm going to be
in New York City

00:03:35.067 --> 00:03:37.200
and I'm going to be walking
looking at, like, don't look up.

00:03:37.200 --> 00:03:38.667
I'm walking,
looking at buildings like that,

00:03:38.667 --> 00:03:40.033
because I don't want to look up.
I don't want to look like

00:03:40.033 --> 00:03:41.634
a tourist!
That's all the things I heard

00:03:41.634 --> 00:03:42.834
when I came in from Texas.

00:03:42.834 --> 00:03:44.067
Like, "Don't look up.
They're going to rob you.

00:03:44.067 --> 00:03:45.834
They're going to mug you."
-[ Laughs ]

00:03:45.834 --> 00:03:47.200
-I know, yeah.
-So, I'm like waiting

00:03:47.200 --> 00:03:49.734
in the hotel. You come get me,
then I'll leave

00:03:49.734 --> 00:03:52.767
because I got somebody with me
to help me if I needed help.

00:03:52.767 --> 00:03:56.067
And it was intimidating.
I hear somebody's laughing.

00:03:56.067 --> 00:03:57.400
-Yeah, that's --

00:03:57.400 --> 00:04:00.601
-I'm being honest. I was scared
to -- It was scary.

00:04:00.601 --> 00:04:03.734
-But I think you're good
at doing things

00:04:03.734 --> 00:04:05.267
that are a little challenging.

00:04:05.267 --> 00:04:07.367
I think that you like
a good challenge.

00:04:07.367 --> 00:04:09.000
-Yes.
-Even like going into

00:04:09.000 --> 00:04:10.334
"Good Morning America".

00:04:10.334 --> 00:04:13.200
Like, you don't know
how to host a morning show,

00:04:13.200 --> 00:04:15.033
but yet you hit it
out of the park.

00:04:15.033 --> 00:04:16.234
You're fantastic at it.

00:04:16.234 --> 00:04:17.501
-Thank you. Thank you.
You know what?

00:04:17.501 --> 00:04:19.400
That was -- that was more
intimidating

00:04:19.400 --> 00:04:20.667
than being in a locker room

00:04:20.667 --> 00:04:23.067
with the big old dudes
walking in there, man.

00:04:23.067 --> 00:04:24.434
-Really?
-Yeah, I was --

00:04:24.434 --> 00:04:26.634
That is the hardest,
most challenging job

00:04:26.634 --> 00:04:28.400
I've ever had.
-Really?

00:04:28.400 --> 00:04:30.334
-Football is challenging
mentally and physically,

00:04:30.334 --> 00:04:33.267
but this -- just
the overall pressure of it,

00:04:33.267 --> 00:04:36.300
you feel it a lot more than
I ever felt on a football field.

00:04:36.300 --> 00:04:38.501
And so it's really hard
to explain,

00:04:38.501 --> 00:04:40.701
but it challenged me in
different ways I never expected,

00:04:40.701 --> 00:04:44.000
because it's not something
that I can say I aspired to do.

00:04:44.000 --> 00:04:46.033
-Yeah.
-But now, I absolutely love it.

00:04:46.033 --> 00:04:48.133
I'm so comfortable there.
I love the news.

00:04:48.133 --> 00:04:49.634
I love politics.

00:04:49.634 --> 00:04:52.234
I love, you know, everything
that's involved with it.

00:04:52.234 --> 00:04:54.534
I just wish the news could be
a little bit happier at times.

00:04:54.534 --> 00:04:55.801
-Yeah
-It's kind of hard.

00:04:55.801 --> 00:04:58.100
It's like 7 AM going,
"Well, you know, today..."

00:04:58.100 --> 00:05:01.000
It's like -- it wears on
your mind some of these things,

00:05:01.000 --> 00:05:04.367
but, I -- I wouldn't
change it for the world.

00:05:04.367 --> 00:05:07.367
My career and where it's taken
has exceeded anything

00:05:07.367 --> 00:05:08.634
I ever imagined, so.

00:05:08.634 --> 00:05:10.367
-I mean your clothing line
has taken off as well.

00:05:10.367 --> 00:05:14.133
Last time we were on, we talked
about -- you were giving suits,

00:05:14.133 --> 00:05:16.601
by the way, to veterans
and helping them get jobs

00:05:16.601 --> 00:05:17.868
when they come out of
the service,

00:05:17.868 --> 00:05:19.234
and I thought that was
an awesome thing you did.

00:05:19.234 --> 00:05:22.367
-Thank you.
-And...

00:05:22.367 --> 00:05:24.467
But this is your --

00:05:24.467 --> 00:05:25.934
Is it --
What do you call, is it...?

00:05:25.934 --> 00:05:27.534
-"Work leisure," we call it.
-Work leisure.

00:05:27.534 --> 00:05:31.033
-You know, there's athleisure,
and then theres tailored,

00:05:31.033 --> 00:05:32.634
which is like suits and ties,

00:05:32.634 --> 00:05:34.567
but now everybody's
like leisure,

00:05:34.567 --> 00:05:36.400
but they're working, because
they're working from home.

00:05:36.400 --> 00:05:38.067
-That's right.
-So what we have,

00:05:38.067 --> 00:05:42.434
we have fitted Polo's, we have,
you know, comfortable joggers,

00:05:42.434 --> 00:05:45.267
and also we have jeans
and everything else.

00:05:45.267 --> 00:05:46.767
Throw a jacket over it,
you can sit in front of

00:05:46.767 --> 00:05:48.267
your Zoom,
you can do your thing.

00:05:48.267 --> 00:05:49.634
Take it off,
you could still hit the streets,

00:05:49.634 --> 00:05:52.968
and not have to change
20 outfits to kind of work now

00:05:52.968 --> 00:05:54.801
and also be at home
and be comfortable.

00:05:54.801 --> 00:05:57.667
-Look at your model poses.
-Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:05:57.667 --> 00:05:59.934
-Now, are any of these,
like, real situations?

00:05:59.934 --> 00:06:02.300
-No.
[ Laughter ]

00:06:02.300 --> 00:06:04.534
All that's in the studio.
It is weird,

00:06:04.534 --> 00:06:07.067
because it's like -- I felt
like "Talladega Nights".

00:06:07.067 --> 00:06:09.267
What do I do with my hands?
You know, you just kind of --

00:06:09.267 --> 00:06:11.033
So, you notice,
I almost have something

00:06:11.033 --> 00:06:12.601
in my hands every time.

00:06:12.601 --> 00:06:14.334
-Yeah.
-And then when you do the suit,

00:06:14.334 --> 00:06:16.567
it's, weird because I saw that
that meme of The Rock

00:06:16.567 --> 00:06:18.000
and he's always fixing
his cuffs.

00:06:18.000 --> 00:06:19.367
-Yeah.
-Like, can The Rock ever fix

00:06:19.367 --> 00:06:21.334
his cuffs?
-[ Laughs ]

00:06:21.334 --> 00:06:22.767
-So now every time
I've got a suit,

00:06:22.767 --> 00:06:24.133
I'm like, "I can't do
the cuff thing --

00:06:24.133 --> 00:06:26.801
that's The Rock's stuff.
That's The Rock's thing.

00:06:26.801 --> 00:06:29.901
But, yeah, it's uncomfortable --
there's, like, a lot of people

00:06:29.901 --> 00:06:31.601
standing around you, like, kind
of like "What do I do?"

00:06:31.601 --> 00:06:32.968
-I know.
-I got to look cool.

00:06:32.968 --> 00:06:34.300
-But I think you always had
a little something,

00:06:34.300 --> 00:06:36.934
because we got a photo
of young Michael Strahan.

00:06:36.934 --> 00:06:40.400
-Oh boy.
-Look at this little stud.

00:06:40.400 --> 00:06:43.334
That's what I'm talking about
right there.

00:06:43.334 --> 00:06:44.968
That's my man right there.

00:06:44.968 --> 00:06:47.767
Walk me --
walk me through this, dude.

00:06:47.767 --> 00:06:50.601
-How cool is he?
-I'm -- I'm the youngest of 6,

00:06:50.601 --> 00:06:53.000
and I was too young
to go to school,

00:06:53.000 --> 00:06:55.000
but my brothers and sisters
had school pictures,

00:06:55.000 --> 00:06:56.767
and I cried and told my mom

00:06:56.767 --> 00:06:58.300
"I want to go to school,
and I want school pictures."

00:06:58.300 --> 00:07:00.868
So she took me to Sears
or something,

00:07:00.868 --> 00:07:02.000
where they used to do the school
pictures --

00:07:02.000 --> 00:07:03.167
-Yes, of course.
-Where you can get the pictures.

00:07:03.167 --> 00:07:05.300
And that was considered
my school picture,

00:07:05.300 --> 00:07:07.968
even though I wasn't in school,
and you could tell I was happy.

00:07:07.968 --> 00:07:09.734
-That's a good mom.
-Because the gap is just

00:07:09.734 --> 00:07:12.467
in awful display.

00:07:12.467 --> 00:07:14.767
-You're going to have to
add this to the MSX line.

00:07:14.767 --> 00:07:16.067
Leisure suits.
-The lei--

00:07:16.067 --> 00:07:19.000
That's polyester. Don't get near
a flame, you going up.

00:07:19.000 --> 00:07:20.467
-This is my man,
Michael Strahan.

00:07:20.467 --> 00:07:23.200
Everybody give it up --
in studio.

00:07:23.200 --> 00:07:26.467
Check out his line of
MSX clothing available now.

